{"action":"revised","summary":"Wrote the complete first draft of the application from the inventor's disclosure: all ten body files in draft/, the reference-numeral table, and four figure specifications (system block diagram, connector-station detail, edge-controller detail, and a process flow of the allocation interval plus the staged branch-protection sequence). Claim set is 20 claims in three statutory classes: system (1 to 14), method (15 to 19), and non-transitory computer-readable medium (20). Important caveat on prior art: prior_art/ contains only INDEX.md and that index lists no references, so the art you gave me may be incomplete and this draft contains no citations. The Background states the technical problems the invention solves in neutral terms, without attributing any teaching to a document I was not given. If a search is run later, the Background is written so that specific references can be cited into it without restructuring the argument.","reasoning":["Claim 1 recites the relationship the disclosure itself calls essential, and nothing more: dual sensing (per-connector current sensors plus a branch current sensor upstream of every connector contactor), allocation from an available charging current obtained by subtracting a measured non-charging branch load and a configurable safety reserve, minimum sustaining current first then energy-deficit distribution, pilot transmission with verification at the connector current sensor, and the staged response of a reduced pilot command followed, if the measured branch current does not respond within a verification interval, by opening contactors one at a time in a stored shedding order. The one-at-a-time ordered shedding gated on a persisting upstream measurement is the limitation doing the real technical work: it bounds branch current by an action wholly inside the controller's power path rather than by a command the vehicle may ignore, and it removes only as much load as the measurement shows to be necessary.","Deliberately kept out of claim 1: the reclose permissives, the welded-contactor detection, the offline token, the tamper-evident record sequence, the commissioning mapping, and the arithmetic for deriving the non-charging load. Each is a separate protective or operational feature that a competitor could omit while still practising the core loop, so each became a dependent claim rather than scope given away for free.","The dependent ladder is graduated rather than decorative. Claim 2 is the next-narrowest fallback, adding the concrete derivation of the non-charging branch load as branch current minus the sum of connector currents. Claims 3 and 4 add the three-condition reclose permissive and the independently drawn randomized delay. Claims 5 and 6 add welded-contactor detection against a leakage threshold and the isolation response. Claims 7 to 9 cover offline operation and the hash-chained meter sequence. Claim 10 covers the commissioning mapping. Claims 11 to 13 cover the sustaining-current behaviour and the closed-loop verification response. Claim 14 covers the polyphase case. Each is a feature that can be argued on its own.","Statutory classes: system, method, and non-transitory medium, because the equipment maker, the site operator running the process, and a firmware supplier are different parties.","The description was widened past the bare disclosure only where a claim term needed enabling support, and always as disclosed alternatives rather than invented facts: the energy-deficit value is described functionally with three example ways to accumulate it, all sharing the property the allocation actually uses; the shedding order, safety reserve, leakage threshold, and interval lengths are described as stored configurable values without inventing numbers; the polyphase branch sensor and the isolated bus implementations are given as alternatives.","One behaviour that crept into an early pass of the commissioning section, operating the local fault indicator when the sensed channel disagrees with the commanded connector, was removed. The disclosure says only that the association is not stored unless the channels agree, and I did not want an unsupported behaviour in the specification.","Figures were split to respect the eight-numeral-per-sheet cap without crowding: FIG. 1 carries the system-level numerals (8), FIG. 2 the connector-station detail (6), FIG. 3 the controller detail (7), and FIG. 4 the eight process steps. No section views were used, so no cutting-plane annotation was needed."],"changes":["draft/01-title.md: title naming the edge controller, the dual-sensed closed-loop allocation, and the ordered contactor shedding.","draft/02-cross-reference.md: Not applicable, per the filing notes (no priority claim).","draft/03-government-support.md: Not applicable, per the filing notes.","draft/04-field.md: field of the disclosure, current allocation among connectors on an aggregate-current-limited branch.","draft/05-background.md: the technical problems, advisory nature of control-pilot signalling, varying non-charging branch load, equal division perpetuating unequal service, welded contactors, loss of the site link, and unverified installation wiring maps. No citations, because prior_art/INDEX.md lists no references.","draft/06-summary.md: summary of the system, the allocation process, the branch current sensor as an independent safety input, recovery, welded-contactor isolation, offline authorization, and commissioning mapping, plus the three statutory embodiments.","draft/07-drawings.md: brief description of FIGS. 1 to 4.","draft/08-detailed-description.md: full description with numerals 100 to 136 and steps 200 to 214, including the technical explanation of why the staged pilot-then-shed sequence is needed, why the minimum sustaining current is never partially assigned, why the randomized recovery delay is drawn per contactor, why a welded contactor forces isolation of the remaining contactors, and why the mapping must be confirmed on the same sensors the allocation later relies on.","draft/09-claims.md: 20 claims. Claim 1 system, claims 2 to 14 dependents, claim 15 method, claims 16 to 19 dependents, claim 20 non-transitory computer-readable medium.","draft/10-abstract.md: 149-word abstract covering the system and the staged protection sequence.","draft/numerals.md: 24 numerals, each used once against one part name.","figures/fig-01.md: system block diagram, numerals 100, 102, 104, 106, 108, 110, 112, 114.","figures/fig-02.md: enlarged first connector station, numerals 106, 110, 120, 122, 124, 126.","figures/fig-03.md: enlarged edge controller, numerals 100, 106, 108, 130, 132, 134, 136.","figures/fig-04.md: process flow, numerals 200, 202, 204, 206, 208, 210, 212, 214."],"prior_art_strategy":"prior_art/ contains only INDEX.md, and that index lists no references, so there was nothing to read, nothing to cite, and no document whose combination I could inspect. The draft therefore contains no citations anywhere, which is the correct handling of an empty index: every listed reference must be addressed, and none are listed. Because I could not clear the claims against actual documents, I built the claim set so that the distinguishing work is done by features the disclosure itself identifies as the invention rather than by generic recitations. Claim 1 turns on the measured upstream branch current governing a power-path action, ordered one-at-a-time contactor opening in a stored order, triggered only by persistence of the over-limit measurement through a verification interval after a reduced pilot command, combined with per-connector closed-loop verification and deficit-ordered allocation of a measured available charging current. Terminology was chosen to be accurate for this invention rather than borrowed from generic load-management vocabulary: branch limit, available charging current, minimum sustaining current, energy-deficit value, stored shedding order, verification interval, randomized recovery delay. The Background sets out, before it is needed, the technical reason each of these exists, in particular that a control-pilot value is a command and not a measurement, so that a later argument over any reference that allocates from commanded values has a written foundation. The dependent ladder gives distinct retreat positions (measured non-charging load derivation, three-condition reclose permissive, leakage-threshold welded-contactor isolation, signed offline token, hash-chained meter records, sensor-confirmed commissioning mapping) in case a search later turns up art against the broadest claim. If you run a search, the strongest candidates to check are dynamic load management for multi-outlet EVSE, contactor welding detection in charging equipment, and OCPP-style offline authorization caching.","questions":[],"answer":"The complete draft is written. draft/ holds all ten body sections, draft/numerals.md holds 24 numerals each bound to a single part name, and figures/ holds four self-contained drawing specifications (fig-01 system block diagram, fig-02 connector-station detail, fig-03 edge-controller detail, fig-04 process flow), each within the 2800-character limit and the eight-numeral-per-sheet cap.

Claim 1 is a system claim reciting the relationship the disclosure identifies as essential: per-connector current sensing and control-pilot signalling, an upstream branch current sensor, allocation of an available charging current obtained by subtracting a measured non-charging branch load and a configurable safety reserve from the branch limit, minimum sustaining current assigned first and the remainder distributed by energy deficit, each assigned limit sent on the pilot and verified at the connector current sensor, and, when the measured branch current stays above the branch limit after a reduced pilot command and a verification interval, opening contactors one at a time in a stored shedding order. Claim 15 is the corresponding method and claim 20 a non-transitory medium. Thirteen system dependents and four method dependents give a graduated fallback ladder: measured non-charging load derivation, three-condition reclose permissive, per-contactor randomized delay, leakage-threshold welded-contactor detection and isolation with a local fault indicator, signed offline token and last accepted branch limit, refusal to start unauthorized new sessions offline, hash-chained meter records, sensor-confirmed commissioning mapping, sustaining-current and verification behaviours, and the polyphase case.

One thing to know before filing: prior_art/ contains only an index that lists no references. I had no art to write around and the draft contains no citations, so the art behind this draft may be incomplete. The Background is written so that references found by a later search can be cited into it without restructuring the argument, and it already sets out, in the specification where a later argument needs it, why a control-pilot value is a command rather than a measurement and why the staged pilot-then-shed sequence follows from that."}

{"action":"revised","summary":"The filing gate reported one mechanical failure: the abstract measured 153 words against the 150-word limit of 37 CFR 1.72(b). I rewrote draft/10-abstract.md to 142 whitespace words, which is 146 words under the stricter convention that splits hyphenated compounds, so it clears the limit under either counting rule with margin. No other file needed a change: the numeral table, drawing descriptions, detailed description, claims, and the four figure briefs were already synchronized and remain so, which I re-verified rather than assumed. No drawing finding was raised, so no figure brief was loosened, shortened, or regenerated, and no disclosed part, numeral, or embodiment was removed.","reasoning":["The failure was reproducible and its cause identifiable: the prior abstract was 149 words by whitespace and contained exactly 4 hyphenated compounds (electric-vehicle, control-pilot twice, non-charging), which accounts precisely for the reported 153. The gate therefore counts hyphenated compounds as multiple words, so I drafted against that stricter rule rather than against whitespace counting, and confirmed both counts before returning.","I cut length from redundancy rather than from substance. The abstract still carries every element that identifies the invention: the branch limit lower than the sum of connector ratings, the per-connector contactor, connector current sensor, and control-pilot interface, the branch current sensor upstream of the contactors, the subtraction of the measured non-charging branch load and safety reserve to obtain the available charging current, minimum sustaining current first then energy-deficit distribution, transmission of each assigned limit with verification of the resulting connector current, and the staged response of a reduced pilot command followed by ordered one-at-a-time contactor shedding.","An intermediate wording of mine had generalized two claim terms to save words, writing 'a pilot interface' and 'a measured load that is not charging'. Once the count showed headroom I restored 'control-pilot interface' and 'non-charging branch load', because the abstract should use the same terms as the claims and description, and the paraphrase bought nothing.","I kept the shedding termination condition, 'until branch current is below that limit', even though it costs seven words. That clause is what distinguishes shedding that removes only as much load as the measurement shows to be necessary from wholesale disconnection, and the abstract is the first thing a searcher reads. The result still leaves four words of margin under the strict count.","I re-verified the rest of the package instead of assuming it was untouched: all 24 numerals appear identically in the detailed description, in draft/numerals.md, and across the figure briefs, with no numeral present in one and absent from another; all four figure briefs remain at or below 2800 characters; and there are no em dashes, placeholders, or drafting notes in draft/ or figures/.","A scan for the word 'confirm' returns hits in the commissioning passage and the meter-record passage. I inspected each one and left them: they describe the controller confirming which sensed channel responds and the remote management system confirming that an uploaded run is complete. They are technical operations of the invention, not questions to the inventor or requests for confirmation from a reader.","No claim, description, numeral definition, or embodiment was altered to accommodate the gate. The failure was an abstract-length rule and was fixed within the abstract."],"changes":["draft/10-abstract.md: rewritten to satisfy 37 CFR 1.72(b). Now 142 words by whitespace and 146 words when hyphenated compounds are split, down from 149 and 153 respectively. Content is unchanged in substance and retains the claim terminology 'control-pilot interface' and 'non-charging branch load' as well as the shedding termination condition."],"prior_art_strategy":"Unchanged by this turn, and the caveat from the previous turn still stands. prior_art/ contains only INDEX.md, and that index lists no references, so there is nothing to cite and the draft contains no citations anywhere. That is the correct handling of an empty index rather than an omission: the rule that every listed reference must be addressed is satisfied vacuously when none are listed. Because the claims could not be cleared against actual documents, the distinguishing work in claim 1 is carried by features the disclosure itself identifies as essential: the measured upstream branch current governing a power-path action, ordered one-at-a-time contactor opening in a stored order triggered only by persistence of the over-limit measurement through a verification interval after a reduced pilot command, combined with per-connector closed-loop verification and deficit-ordered allocation of a measured available charging current. The Background continues to state, in the specification where a later argument would need it, why a control-pilot value is a command and not a measurement, so references found by a future search can be cited into that argument without restructuring it. The dependent ladder still supplies distinct retreat positions: measured non-charging load derivation, three-condition reclose permissive, leakage-threshold welded-contactor isolation, signed offline token, hash-chained meter records, and sensor-confirmed commissioning mapping. If a search is run, the areas worth checking first remain dynamic load management for multi-outlet charging equipment, contactor welding detection, and cached offline authorization.","questions":[],"answer":"The gate's single finding is fixed. The abstract was 153 words under the gate's counting convention, which splits hyphenated compounds; draft/10-abstract.md is now 142 words by whitespace and 146 under that stricter rule, clearing the 150-word limit of 37 CFR 1.72(b) either way.

The rewrite preserved the invention and the application's own vocabulary. The abstract still recites the branch limit below the sum of the connector ratings, the per-connector contactor, connector current sensor, and control-pilot interface, the branch current sensor upstream of the contactors, the subtraction of a measured non-charging branch load and a safety reserve to obtain the available charging current, assignment of the minimum sustaining current followed by energy-deficit distribution of the remainder, transmission of each assigned limit with verification of the resulting connector current, and the staged response in which a reduced pilot command is followed by opening contactors one at a time in a stored shedding order until branch current is below the limit.

Nothing else required editing, and I confirmed that rather than assuming it. All 24 numerals match across the detailed description, draft/numerals.md, and the four figure briefs. Every figure brief remains within the 2800-character limit. There are no em dashes, placeholders, drafting notes, or questions in draft/ or figures/. Claims, description, numeral table, and figure briefs are untouched and mutually consistent, and since no drawing defect was raised, no sheet needed regeneration."}
